  "subtitle": "A wooden box that dispensed glass marbles — one per voicemail — and let you listen by dropping them into a slot. No screen, no keyboard, and a decade ahead of the Internet of Things.",
  "why_scout_cares": "Durrell Bishop's 1992 RCA graduation project is the single most cited non-academic precedent for tangible user interfaces. It crystallized, in one physical prototype, the idea that digital information could be embodied in graspable objects — each marble a message, its position and presence carrying all the meaning. Pick up a marble, drop it in the play slot, and hear the voicemail. Drop it in a different slot to call the person back. Hand it to a friend to share a message. The number of marbles in the tray told you at a glance how many messages waited. This was what Cory Doctorow called an 'Internet of Things answering machine from 1992' — physical tokens coupled with networked digital information, a decade before the term IoT existed. Ishii & Ullmer cited Bishop explicitly in their foundational 'Tangible Bits' paper (CHI 1997). The 'token + constraint' interaction pattern — marbles (tokens) placed in specific slots (constraints) — was later formalized in a canonical ACM TOCHI paper (2005). The prototype was built from wood and off-the-shelf electronics at the RCA's Computer Related Design programme, founded by Gillian Crampton Smith — the same programme that would later spawn the Interaction Design Institute Ivrea, where Arduino was invented. Bishop went on to IDEO, Apple's Advanced Technology Group, and Interval Research. The marble machine is a student project that changed the direction of an entire field.",
